  "surprise": "591 (585/605/584) vs 631: every seed below the incumbent's worst seed (609). Pretraining matched 115 on the cap-6 distribution (val 0.082-0.084, held-out greedy 0.978-0.984 vs 0.9815) but the (B,H,T,T) prefix mask cost 17% of the steps (3480 vs 4204; predicted 3-8%). The gap opened at RL round 1, before any fine-tuning: sample accuracy on the 7+ targets 0.18/0.21/0.27 vs 115's 0.32, 568/646/690 targets solved vs 718, and it never closed (r4 1063/1085/1057 vs 1131); eval losses sit at lengths 9-11 (s0 316/64/13 vs 339/78/20). So a bidirectional theorem encoding fit the training prompts equally well but transferred worse to the longer, more-premise prompts of the RL targets. Candidate causes: the NoPE heads have no order signal at all inside a bidirectional prefix (causal NoPE gets order from the mask), and the 2x right-hand ALiBi slope changes prompt attention beyond training lengths; the step loss alone is unlikely to explain a round-1 accuracy drop of a third. Cached decoding matched the full forward to 3e-7 in a CPU check, so this is not a cache bug.",
